menu "STEF Board"

    comment "Single-wire UART to every TMC2209"

    config STEF_TMC_UART_NUM
        int "UART peripheral"
        range 1 2
        default 1
        help
            UART carrying the TMC2209 link. Not 0: that one belongs to the
            console on boards that still have one.

    config STEF_TMC_UART_TX_GPIO
        int "UART TX GPIO"
        range 0 48
        default 42
        help
            Goes to PDN_UART through the series resistor. TX and RX are tied
            together at the driver, so the ESP32 hears its own bytes back.
            That echo is expected: it is what the library checks to notice a
            collision on the wire.

    config STEF_TMC_UART_RX_GPIO
        int "UART RX GPIO"
        range 0 48
        default 41
        help
            Goes to PDN_UART directly.

    config STEF_TMC_BAUD
        int "UART baud"
        range 9600 500000
        default 115200
        help
            The TMC2209 recovers the rate from the sync byte, so this is the
            ESP32's choice rather than the driver's. 115200 is unambiguous on
            a hand-wired board; raise it once the carrier board exists.

    config STEF_TMC_UART_TRACE
        bool "Log every byte on the driver bus"
        default y
        help
            Logs what went out and what came back for every transaction, as a
            log frame the PC receives like any other.

            The question a silent bus raises is whether anything came back at
            all, and no status code answers it: a bad echo says the bytes did
            not match, not what did arrive. Costs a log line per datagram, so
            turn it off once the wiring is settled.

    comment "Pulse generation"

    config STEF_STEPGEN_MAX_PPS
        int "Fastest rate a driver may be asked for, in pulses per second"
        range 1000 300000
        default 100000
        help
            Not a limit of the RMT peripheral, which reaches further. It is
            what the ramp arithmetic sustains with every driver on the board
            running at once, since each one refills its channel from an
            interrupt. A rate above this is refused rather than clamped: a move
            that ran slower than believed is a position error.

            Pulses, not steps. One full step holds up to 256 of them.

    comment "Driver 0"

    config STEF_DRV0_NAME
        string "Name"
        default "capstan"
        help
            What the PC calls this driver. Name it after what it moves, so the
            name survives a rewiring.

    config STEF_DRV0_ADDR
        int "Address (MS1/MS2 straps)"
        range 0 3
        default 0
        help
            MS1 is bit 0 and MS2 is bit 1. Both pins carry internal pull-downs,
            so a driver whose straps are left open answers on 0.

    config STEF_DRV0_ENN_GPIO
        int "ENN GPIO"
        range -1 48
        default 40
        help
            Active low: high disables the power stage. -1 if not wired, which
            makes every call naming it a clean refusal.

            Wants a pull-up to VIO as well. The pin carries no internal one, so
            the level while the ESP32 is in reset or being flashed is whatever
            the board gives it.

    config STEF_DRV0_DIR_GPIO
        int "DIR GPIO"
        range -1 48
        default -1

    config STEF_DRV0_STEP_GPIO
        int "STEP GPIO"
        range -1 48
        default -1

    config STEF_DRV0_DIAG_GPIO
        int "DIAG GPIO"
        range -1 48
        default -1
        help
            Input. Driver error or StallGuard, depending on GCONF.

endmenu
